It depends on whether you think A should be centered.
You need to have something to identify the linear
predictor's location, so A and B can't both float.
A better alternative is to have an intercept and then
let A and B have centered priors.

You want to do the same thing for B, by the way.

Here's what you have (I find those spaces before indexes and
arguments to functions very hard to read):

	for (i in 1 : N) {

		eta [i] <- - elasticity * log1p (distancex [i] / a) +
			A [origin [i]] + B [destination [i]];

- Bob

