I just wondered whether the results were repeatable with different
random number seeds, which changes the initial position and sequence
of draws.

For the simple uniform interval model, it's not going to make a big
difference because any random init is already a reasonable draw from
the posterior.

- Bob

> On Apr 10, 2015, at 1:51 PM, Kevin Van Horn <ke...@ksvanhorn.com> wrote:
>
> I don't remember seeing any question about seed and init-based variation. Could you explain?
>
> On Wednesday, April 8, 2015 at 5:18:44 PM UTC-6, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> I'm still not sure it's not just seed and init-based variation.
> We never heard back from Kevin about that.
>
> - Bob
>
> > On Apr 9, 2015, at 3:52 AM, Michael Betancourt <betan...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> > Yes, it is very bad because the integrator will inevitably diverge out in those tails no matter how small the step size is made.  But, again, the additional prior/likelihood contributions should fix all of that.  I'm still a bit surprised that a model would be sensitive to these differences -- it must have been really diffuse.
> >
> >
> > On Apr 8, 2015, at 5:58 PM, Ben Goodrich <goodri...@gmail.com> wrote:
> >
> >> On Wednesday, April 8, 2015 at 10:14:25 AM UTC-4, Michael Betancourt wrote:
> >> Now _both_ of these potential energies yield well-behaved trajectories
> >> for HMC (HMC with either a static or dynamic integration time is
> >> geometrically ergodic for both!).  The problem with the logit-induced
> >> potential energy is that the period of the trajectory increases out in
> >> the tails while the period of the inverse-Phi-induced trajectories is
> >> constant.  What this means is that out in the tails the logit-induced
> >> trajectories need to evolve for a much longer time to cover the same
> >> distance, which is why the number of leapfrog steps increased
> >> relative to the inverse-Phi trajectories.
> >>
> >> Is it somehow bad to induce slightly more curvature than 0.5 * x^2? Otherwise, Phi_approx() is going to be much faster than Phi() for 230000 transformations.
> >>
> >>
> >> Ben
