Also,

3.  Use stronger priors.  In many applications we’ve seen, it’s not so hard to add good prior info that makes everything run better.

Also, with stronger priors it still makes sense to follow Bob’s advice #2 and use reasonable inits.

> On Apr 10, 2015, at 11:15 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
