From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Wed Apr 22 18:20:36 2015
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Wed, 22 Apr 2015 18:20:36 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] more efficient way to write PPCA model?
Message-ID: <DYofzSAz1sk.cIUjsWwhAv0J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <DYofzSAz1sk.w4cEM5cziN0J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <DYofzSAz1sk.w4cEM5cziN0J@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/DYofzSAz1sk/m/cIUjsWwhAv0J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Without worrying about what the model's doing, I can give
you some performance tips.  The basic idea is that you
don't want to create a diagonal covariance matrix and then
sample from a multivariate normal.

> On Apr 22, 2015, at 6:02 PM, Lei Li <lil...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
>
> I am experimenting with Tipping & Bishop's PPCA model. However, two versions I tried in Stan seem slow. Is there better way to write an efficient PPCA model?
> Thanks!
> Lei
>
> PPCA version 1:
> data {
>   int<lower=1> N;              // num datapoints
>   int<lower=1> D;              // num dimension
>   int<lower=1> K;              // num basis
>   real<lower=0> sigma1;
>   //real<lower=0> sigma2;
>   real<lower=0> sigma3;
>   real<lower=0> sigma4;
>   real y[D,N];
> }
> transformed data {
>   matrix[K,K] Sigma;
>   matrix[D,D] Sigma2;
>   vector<lower=0>[K] diag_elem;
>   vector<lower=0>[K] zr_vec;
>   vector<lower=0>[D] diag_elem2;
>   vector<lower=0>[D] zr_vec2;
>   for (k in 1:K) zr_vec[k] <- 0;
>   for (k in 1:K) diag_elem[k] <- sigma3;
>   Sigma <- diag_matrix(diag_elem);
>   for (d in 1:D) zr_vec2[d] <- 0;
>   for (d in 1:D) diag_elem2[d] <- sigma1;
>   Sigma2 <- diag_matrix(diag_elem2);
> }
> parameters {
>   matrix[K,D] A;            // basis
>   vector[K] x[N];           // coefficients
> }
> model {
>
>   //vector[K] x[N];
>
>   for (i in 1:N)
>       x[i] ~ multi_normal(zr_vec, Sigma);

Because Sigma = diag_matrix(diag_elem) and diag_elem is just copies of sigma3,
and zr_vec is all zeroes, this is much more efficiently done as:

  to_vector(x) ~ normal(0,sigma3);

>
>   for (k in 1:K)
>     A[k] ~ multi_normal(zr_vec2, Sigma2);

Same again

>
>   for (i in 1:N)
>     //y[i] ~ multi_normal(A * x[i], Sigma);
>     for (d in 1:D)
>       //y[d,i] ~ normal(dot_product(row(A, d), x[i]), sigma4);
>       increment_log_prob(normal_log(y[d,i], dot_product(col(A, d), x[i]), sigma4));

If you can reduce this to matrix algebra with A and x and then
vectorize, it'll be a lot faster.

The second version is similar in terms of Stan coding.

- Bob

