> >
> > a) x, as was done in the original article by Tipping & Bishop (see section 3.1 http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/67218/bishop-ppca-jrss.pdf)
> > b) A, see article: Probabilistic Non-linear Principal Component Analysis with
> > Gaussian Process Latent Variable Models by Neil Lawrence (link: http://jmlr.org/papers/volume6/lawrence05a/lawrence05a.pdf)
> >
> > Both will give simple mv-Gaussian likelihood with certain mean&covariance matrix. By doing option b), we can easily see that PCA correspond to Gaussian process with linear covariance function (and by changing the covariance functions we can get non-linear dimension reduction models etc, these are called Gaussian Process latent variable models, GP-LVM)
